WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.280
So, customer opens up their banking app and types.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:14.520
My card hasn't arrived yet. How can I track it?
Where is it?

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:20.680
They want to know where their card is and the first
step is getting that question to the right place.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:27.080
So I built a classifier that identifies what the
customer needs and returns a routing decision

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:33.140
to a support queue, whether it be a person or an
application.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:44.540
So here, the classifier is recognizing a card
arrival question with 98% confidence.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:54.380
That clears the routing threshold and so the result
is marked for a matching intent queue.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:59.880
Once it's connected to the support system, that
decision could open a delivery status

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:08.280
workflow or direct the request to a team handling
missing cards.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.080
Now the customer says something different.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.160
They say something's wrong with my account.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:25.360
That is a pretty vague question, that's a pretty
vague request.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.720
It's not enough detail to choose a next step on.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:37.120
And so the classifier with not too low confidence
but not high enough to make a decision off

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:44.880
of returns that it needs human review, along with
its leading alternatives and reasons

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.320
behind the review request.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:57.200
The system still scores the banking categories, the
essentials so to say, but none of them

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:03.560
will clear the confidence threshold and a predicted
label will not trigger automatic

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:12.400
routing because of the confidence threshold never
being cleared.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:20.920
On the actual banking questions, the policy accepted
about 42% for automatic routing.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:29.920
Within that group, it got a 99.46% accuracy.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.280
That's your operating trade off.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:42.820
You get to automate a decent chunk of the work while
keeping a clear set review path for everyone else.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:51.000
So under the hood, we're looking at a Hugging Face
data set called BANKING77.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:58.880
And so each decision point had its own subset of
data.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:09.160
So I compared a TF-IDF baseline with a pre-trained
text encoder.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:16.520
And then I trained two different encoder
configurations on some Kaggle GPUs.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:21.400
Now the stronger encoder ended up getting the better
validation results.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:30.960
I also tested what happens when the training hits a
bug, stops unexpectedly or crashes.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.720
We have forced crash tests.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:40.440
And so what we're doing is we restore the model, the
optimizer and the training position from a
checkpoint.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:48.000
And then we verify and validate that the recovered
run matches the uninterrupted training.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.960
This makes our future work recoverable and
repeatable.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:59.240
And so we need to make our decisions traceable.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:12.360
And every prediction includes its model version, its
confidence, its routing reason, and latency within
it.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:18.360
Evaluation reports travel with verified artifact
hashes and reporting adapters, support local

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.840
checks, and Databricks imports.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:27.320
And so we have a way to have the engineer who is
managing this and watching and building

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:34.360
telemetry about this, a way to investigate decisions
that go awry and compare model releases

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:40.080
as they upgrade and iterate and make better.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:46.240
So this project connects model training to business
applications.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:58.760
It takes decisions and requests, chooses our route,
and makes uncertainty visible.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:08.880
It demonstrates the engineering behind an AI support
tool, applied AI at its finest.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:14.880
Measured behavior, recoverable tracing, and a clear
interface where any application can

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.550
integrate with. And that's what I built.
